From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:47:01 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] Windows 10 Mobile: Chrome's browser layer in one process, in
 an app

What Chrome's own startup and first pages need on the phone, from a
research pass over //chrome as it would run there (single-process, inside
the engine DLL, no window system):
- //chrome/common leaves out Chrome's delay-load failure hook: the
  engine's turns a missing function into a failing call, Chrome's would
  end the process (and the two would not link together).
- ProcessSingleton::Create is true at once: one app instance, Chrome a
  DLL in it, and no message window to find or be found through (it
  CHECKed one).
- The status tray exists but shows nowhere, with icons that do nothing:
  several owners dereference the tray and its icons (WebUSB/HID device
  icons, Glic), so it is no longer null.
- No incognito, guest or second profile: the one in-process renderer
  serves one storage partition (RenderProcessHostImpl CHECKs).
- A single-process renderer is not a PDF renderer: Supermium's
  pdf::IsPdfRenderer() says so of it, which took DOM storage and the
  default CSP away from every page.
- The PDF viewer finds its PDF as its own child frame (the one process is
  never marked as a PDF renderer's).
- Web Serial goes without a device monitor instead of observing null.
- No jump list; no startup metrics (default browser via shell32, IFEO,
  WMI, TPM key operations), which nobody reads in this build.
- Printing: no print compositor (it would set up fonts and discardable
  memory for the whole process again), one page a sheet (N-up runs
  PDFium a second time in-process and CHECKs), and the printing and PDF
  services keep the renderer's discardable allocator.
- URLs of other apps, downloaded files and their folders go to a handler
  the embedder sets (platform_util::SetW10mOpenHandler): the shell opens
  them with the Windows Runtime launcher.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
